      "answer_text": "Weak flippers on your Stern Godzilla LE might stem from several issues. Based on the Stern manual and troubleshooting guides, here\u0027s how to diagnose and fix them:\n\n### Steps to Troubleshoot:\n\n1. **Inspect Flipper Components**  \n   - Open the playfield and check the flipper coil, plunger, coil stop, and coil sleeve for dirt, friction, or wear. Clean any dirty parts, and replace worn coil stops or sleeves [[cite:2]].\n\n2. **Verify Flipper Power Settings**  \n   - Use the diagnostics menu to ensure the flipper coil power is set to the recommended level. Low power settings can lead to weak performance [[cite:2]].\n\n3. **Check Coil Connections**  \n   - Inspect the wires and solder points on the flipper coils for looseness or burns. Tighten any loose connections, and redo poor soldering [[cite:2]].\n\n4. **Test the Flipper Button Switch**  \n   - Check the flipper button switches on the cabinet for dirt or wear. Clean them or replace the switches if they appear degraded [[cite:2]].\n\n5. **Investigate Node Board Power**  \n   - The flipper coil is powered via a node board. Use the diagnostics tools to check the node board, as issues here can cause weak flippers [[cite:2]].\n\n### Advanced Help:\nIf the problem persists after these steps, check for voltage drops or consult Stern technical support. Here\u2019s their technical manual for detailed guidance: Stern Godzilla LE Manual.\n\nMake sure to disconnect power before touching components. Let me know if you\u0027d like help with any of these steps!",
