      ],
      "acceptable_refusal": false,
      "predicted_refusal": false,
      "answer_text": "I couldn\u0027t find specific troubleshooting guidance for **Galactic Tank Force**, but here are some general steps to follow for pinball machines that don\u0027t boot or are unresponsive:\n\n1. **Check Power Connection**:\n   - Ensure the power cord is securely plugged into the wall outlet.\n   - Confirm that the outlet is functional by testing with another device.\n\n2. **Verify the Power Switch Position**:\n   - Make sure the main power switch is on. It\u0027s typically located underneath the cabinet or on the back of the machine.\n\n3. **Inspect the Fuses**:\n   - Access the fuse panel (usually in the backbox or cabinet) and test each fuse with a multimeter for continuity. Replace any blown fuses with ones that match the specs in the game\u2019s manual.\n\n4. **Examine Internal Connections**:\n   - Look for loose or disconnected wires inside the backbox or near the power supply.\n\n5. **Inspect the Power Supply Unit (PSU)**:\n   - Check for visible damage like scorch marks or bulging capacitors.\n   - Test its output voltage with a multimeter to ensure it meets specifications.\n\n6. **Test the On/Off Switch**:\n   - Use a multimeter to confirm the machine\u2019s on/off switch is operational.\n\n7. **Check Node Boards (if used)**:\n   - Inspect modern node boards for signs of burnt components or disconnected wiring.\n\n8. **Contact American Pinball Support**:\n   - If these steps don\u0027t resolve the issue, consult the manufacturer\u2019s manual or directly contact American Pinball support for more assistance.\n\nFor **Galactic Tank Force-specific information**, the OPDB page might be helpful, or you can reach out to other players for advice. Let me know if you need further assistance digging into any of these steps!",
